\n\
Scenario: Opponent double-blocks? They can't — Rallier can't block flyers. Only Glider Kids can block.\n\
\n\
Scenario: Opponent doesn't block. I deal 3 damage (1 + 2), opp to 16. I save Octopus Form.\n\
\n\
Actually, do they want to block at all? If they block my 2/3 Glider Kids with theirs, I have Octopus Form to blow them out. If they block the 1/2, I Octopus Form to save it (but their 2/3 survives). Hmm, actually with Octopus Form on First-Time Flyer (1/2 → 2/3), it deals 2 to their 2/3 — their Glider Kids survives with 2 damage. Not a kill.\n\
\n\
Actually, wait — should I consider holding First-Time Flyer back? If I attack with only Glider Kids and they block with their 2/3, I Octopus Form mine to kill theirs and keep mine. That's the blowout. If they don't block, I deal 2.\n\
\n\
If I attack with both, the risk is they block the 1/2 and then either I spend Octopus Form to save it (their blocker survives) or I lose the 1/2. \n\
\n\
Actually let me think about what's best:\n\
- Attack with both: worst case they block 1/2, I either lose flyer or spend Octopus Form for no kill (but save creature). Best case they block Glider Kids → blowout, or no blocks → 3 damage.\n\
- Attack with Glider Kids only: if they block, blowout with Octopus Form. If no block, 2 damage.\n\
\n\
Hmm, but from opponent's perspective, do they know I have a trick? They saw Octopus Form? No, they haven't. They might block the 1/2 to kill it while taking 2 from Glider Kids... no wait, if they block the 1/2, my Glider Kids is unblocked and deals 2.\n\
\n\
Let me reconsider: attacking with both is the aggressive play. Opponent's options:\n\
1. No block: take 3, go to 16.\n\
2. Block Glider Kids (2/3) with theirs: trade — but I have Octopus Form so I kill theirs and keep mine. Even without the trick it's a trade of 2/3 for 2/3.\n\
3. Block First-Time Flyer (1/2) with theirs: kill my 1/2, take 2 from Glider Kids, go to 17. But I have Octopus Form to save the 1/2 — it becomes 2/3, survives, their Glider Kids takes 2 damage (survives until end of turn with damage marked... wait, 2/3 with 2 damage marked survives since 2 < 3).\n\
\n\
So attacking with both dominates — I have the trick to punish blocks. The main \"loss\" is if they block the 1/2 and I have to use my trick to save a 1/2 without killing their blocker. But even then, my 2/3 got through for 2 and I saved my creature.\n\
\n\
Actually, hmm, one more consideration: should I be more patient and build my board first? I have Airbender Ascension in hand which wants lots of creatures entering. My plan: attack now, then postcombat play stuff. I'm at 4 mana. Postcombat I can cast Water Tribe Captain (3/3) and Airbender Ascension... that costs {2}{W} + {1}{W} = 5 mana. I only have 4. So I can play Captain OR Ascension + Momo? Momo is {W} 1/1 flying vigilance. Ascension is {1}{W}. Momo + Ascension = {2}{W} = 3 mana, leaving 1. Or Captain = {2}{W} = 4 mana.\n\
\n\
The previous plan said play Captain (its ETB puts a quest counter on the Ascension I'll deploy next turn). That's fine.\n\
\n\
For combat: attack with both flyers. If they block, Octopus Form punishes. Let me attack with both.\n\
\n\
Actually wait, one more thought — should I save Octopus Form for protection rather than using it in combat? The earlier log said \"Holding Octopus Form since there's no block to punish — saving it as a protection trick.\" Now there might be a block to punish. If they block my 2/3 with theirs, Octopus Form turns a trade into a blowout. That's worth it.\n\
\n\
Decision: attack with both First-Time Flyer and Glider Kids.\n\
\n\
