\n\
Actually, the best target: opponent's Water Tribe Rallier. It's their only creature. If I airbend it, they can recast it for 2 mana, but that's still tempo loss — they spent 2 mana to play it, now need 2 more. Actually airbending is generally used on your own creatures to reuse ETBs. On opponents' creatures, it's temporary removal but they recast cheaply.\n\
\n\
Their board: Water Tribe Rallier 2/2. My board: 3/4 hexproof flyer, 2/3 flyer, Ascension. I'm ahead in the air. Airbending the Rallier doesn't help much — it can't block flyers anyway. Better use: airbend my own Glider Kids? It has no beneficial ETB besides scry 1. Hmm, Momo in hand has leaves-battlefield trigger.\n\
\n\
Actually, wait — the Ascension's goal is to get 4 quest counters. Quest counters come from creatures entering. Airbending my own creature then recasting it gives another ETB = another counter. But airbend exiles and I can cast it for 2 — but I have only 1 untapped land right now (Plains untapped? \"Untapped lands: 1\"). I have Momo ({W}) and Water Tribe Captain ({2}{W}) in hand. With 1 untapped land (Plains presumably), I can cast Momo.\n\
\n\
Hmm, the airbend happens now. If I airbend my Glider Kids, I can cast it for 2 — but I only have 1 mana available. So can't recast this turn. \n\
\n\
Option: Don't airbend anything (it's \"up to one\"). The main value of airbending opponent's creature: tempo. Their Rallier costs them 2 to recast. That keeps their board clear-ish. Rallier can't block flyers, so it doesn't matter for my attacks. But it does waterbend to find creatures — it's a card advantage engine for them. If I airbend it, they lose that engine activation and must spend 2 to recast.\n\
\n\
Actually, recasting it would trigger... nothing for me. Hmm, but wait — quest counters trigger when MY creatures enter. Their recast doesn't help me.\n\
\n\
I think airbending the Rallier is reasonable tempo play: they have 4 lands? They have Island, Island, North Pole Gates — 3 lands. Recasting Rallier for 2 uses most of their mana, slowing their development. That seems good. Alternatively airbend nothing.\n\
\n\
Actually, let me think about the Ascension's end-step ability: at 4+ quest counters, exile up to one target creature I control and return it — that's a blink to reuse ETB (Glider Kids scry) or reset First-Time Flyer... First-Time Flyer is naturally 1/2 with +1/+1 from Lesson in graveyard, but Octopus Form's +1/+1... wait, Octopus Form gives +1/+1 until end of turn only. Wait, but the board shows First-Time Flyer 3/4 and the log said \"stays 2/3 permanently\"? Octopus Form: \"gets +1/+1 and gains hexproof until end of turn.\" Hmm, but board shows 3/4 now during my postcombat main? The boost should have worn off. Unless... the card reference says \"First-Time Flyer 3/4: Flying / gets +1/+1 as long as there's a Lesson card in your graveyard. Hexproof.\" Wait — hexproof isn't in First-Time Flyer's normal text. Oh, maybe Octopus Form is an Aura? No — it's listed as an instant presumably. Actually the reference shows Octopus Form as a spell. Hmm, but why is First-Time Flyer 3/4 with Hexproof now?\n\
\n\
Oh wait — maybe Octopus Form in this set creates a permanent buff? The text shown: \"Target creature you control gets +1/+1 and gains hexproof until end of turn. Untap it.\" That's until end of turn. But we're in turn 4 postcombat main, and the buff was cast turn 4 combat, so it's still active until end of turn 4. Right! Same turn. First-Time Flyer base 1/2 +1/+1 from Lesson (Octopus Form is a Lesson? It must be a Lesson — the reference says \"Lesson cards in your graveyard: 1\") +1/+1 from Octopus Form = 3/4 with hexproof until EOT. After EOT it becomes 2/3.\n\
\n\
OK so decision: airbend target. Choices: my First-Time Flyer (no — loses buffs), my Glider Kids (tapped; airbending resets it, could recast for 2 later, scry again), or their Rallier.\n\
\n\
I think the best play is airbend the Rallier. It's their only creature and a card-advantage engine; forcing them to spend {2} to recast slows them down. They have 3 lands; recasting takes 2 of 3 mana.\n\
