      "reasoning": "The Ascension end-step trigger is repeatable blink value. Blinking Glider Kids is the best target — it untaps it so it's available as a flying blocker on their turn, and its ETB gives me a free scry 1 to dig for action. Blinking First-Time Flyer or the token gains nothing beyond a quest counter.",
      "thinking": "Airbender Ascension's end-step trigger: exile up to one target creature I control, then return it to the battlefield under its owner's control. This blinks a creature, which resets it (removes sickness? No — a creature that enters this turn has summoning sickness, but blinked at end step, it'll be ready next turn anyway).\n\
\n\
Best target: First-Time Flyer or Glider Kids. Glider Kids has an ETB scry 1. First-Time Flyer has no ETB. Blinking Glider Kids gives me a scry 1. Blinking First-Time Flyer — no ETB value, but it would re-enter and... Ascension trigger: \"Whenever a creature you control enters, put a quest counter\" — so the blinked creature re-entering puts a quest counter on Ascension (resetting to 1).\n\
\n\
Wait, important consideration: blinked creature returns, triggering Ascension's counter gain. Ascension resets? Actually it says \"if this has four or more quest counters, exile up to one target creature you control, then return it\" — it doesn't say it removes counters. Let me check: \"At the beginning of your end step, if {this} has four or more quest counters on it, exile up to one target creature you control, then return it to the battlefield under its owner's control.\"\n\
\n\
Hmm, it doesn't remove counters. So it stays at 4, and blinked creature entering puts it to 5. Then next end step it triggers again. So this is a repeatable blink engine.\n\
\n\
Best target: Glider Kids for scry 1 value. Or First-Time Flyer to... no ETB. The Ally Token? No value, and it re-enters but no ETB.\n\
\n\
Actually wait — one consideration: does blinking First-Time Flyer keep the Lesson bonus? First-Time Flyer gets +1/+1 as long as there's a Lesson in graveyard — that's a static ability, not counters, so it stays 2/3 regardless.\n\
\n\
So blink Glider Kids for the scry 1. That's the value play.\n\
\n\
Alternatively, consider: does blinking a creature make it unable to attack next turn? No — it enters at end step of my turn, so by my next turn it's no longer summoning sick.\n\
\n\
Glider Kids is tapped (attacked this turn). Blinking it untaps it, making it available as a blocker during opponent's turn. That's actually valuable defensively too — a 2/3 flying blocker untapped.\n\
\n\
Yes, blink Glider Kids. Scry 1 plus untap.",
