- CR 506.4: \"A permanent is removed from combat if it leaves the battlefield, if its controller changes, if it phases out, if an effect specifically removes it from combat, if it's a creature and it's regenerating...\" Hmm no, regeneration used to remove from combat (not anymore? Regeneration DID remove from combat — actually yes, regeneration still removes the creature from combat). Tapping is NOT on the list. So yes — tapping an attacker after declaration does NOT remove it from combat. It still assigns and deals combat damage. (Famous rules question; answer: tapping doesn't stop the attack. That's why \"tap\" effects like Master of Decoy / Ballynock Trapper must be activated before the declare-attackers step.)\n\
\n\
Great, so even in the worst case under modern rules, attacking wins. The only way attacking fails: (a) engine uses nonstandard \"tapped = removed\" rule, or (b) opponent has some trick with 0 cards in hand — impossible (no cards). Their only in-play interaction is the Patrols and lands (Kyoshi Village/Meditation Pools/North Pole Gates sacrifice to draw a card — wait! Those lands have \"{4}, {T}, Sacrifice: Draw a card.\" Could they draw into an answer? They'd need 4 mana + tap + sac. They have exactly 4 untapped lands (Island, Kyoshi Village, Plains, Plains). Sacrificing Kyoshi Village with 4 mana: tap 3 lands + Village for {4}, sac Village → draw 1 card. Could that card save them? An instant-speed flying blocker or removal? Possible in theory! E.g., a removal spell for my Flyer, or a flash flyer. Hmm, but they're at 3 and it's combat — they'd need to do it during my declare attackers... draw one random card from 20 that answers a 3/3 flyer RIGHT NOW. What could it be? Aang, Swift Savior is exiled (under my Iceberg). Another Aang? Flash creatures that block flyers... It's a draft deck; possible they have something like a removal instant. But it's 1 card off the top of 20 — small chance, and they can try it regardless of what I do (they could do it in my main phase too). I can't prevent it. Attacking forces them to have it NOW.\n\
\n\
Also they could use the draw at instant speed during my combat — if they draw, say, a creature, they can't flash it in unless it has flash. A removal instant could kill/tap my Flyer. That's their only out and it's a ~5-15% rip. Accept the risk — attack.\n\
\n\
Should I also attack with the Ally token? No — as analyzed, only downside (dies to sick Patrol block if Flyer gets neutralized). Keep it back for... hmm, actually is there upside to attacking with the token? If Flyer connects for 3, game over — token irrelevant. If Flyer gets removed by a ripped removal, token gets blocked by sick Patrol and dies, or... also irrelevant-ish. If they rip a removal for the Flyer AND can't block the token... they can block with sick Patrol (2/3 kills 1/1). So no scenario where the token deals the last point. Keep it back (it also chump-blocks on their turn... well, vs their ground guys). Yes, attack with only the Flyer.\n\
\n\
Wait, actually — one more thing. If the opponent draws a card via land-sac in response and it's nothing, whatever. But actually can they even activate Kyoshi Village's ability? It requires {4}, {T}, Sacrifice. Untapped lands: Island, Kyoshi Village, Plains, Plains. Tap all 4 = 4 mana (Village taps for G or W) + sac Village → draw 1. Yes, they can do it. They could also do it before blocks to dig for an instant trick. It's their one prayer. Whatever — attacking is still clearly right. If they rip an answer, so be it; not attacking doesn't improve my position.\n\
\n\
Hmm, also consider: should I cast Tiger-Seal before attacking? No reason to — it doesn't affect this combat (it would enter, trigger Ascension counter, but it's tapped... actually it enters untapped (its self-tap is only at upkeep). Could Tiger-Seal attack this turn? No — summoning sickness. Could it matter as an extra attacker next turn? Ground attacker that taps itself at upkeep — useless offensively. Defensively tapped too. So Tiger-Seal is basically just an Ascension quest counter + potential 3/3 body if I ever draw 2 cards in a turn. Casting it now for {U}: +1 quest counter (9→10). Meh. No harm but no benefit this combat. Actually — could casting it bait the Patrol tap? No, Patrol taps flying attacker, not Tiger-Seal. I'll cast it maybe later; actually why not cast it now (1 mana, triggers counter, adds a body for future double-draw scenarios)? It also gives the opponent more to think about. Eh — it doesn't change this turn's attack. I can cast it after combat too. Postcombat main is fine. Actually, let me cast it NOW precombat? No wait — we're already in declare attackers (the game skipped past my precombat main after I played the Island and passed). Too late; it's declare attackers now. So: attack with the Flyer. Then postcombat, cast Tiger-Seal for the quest counter and future potential body.\n\
\n\
Decision: attack with First-Time Flyer (p103) only.\n\
\n\
Let me write the summary: Turn 12 declare attackers: opponent at 3 life, 0 cards in hand, no flying blockers, Aang exiled under my Iceberg. Their Patrols' tap ability can't remove an attacker from combat once declared (and they left my Flyer untapped through my main phase). Attacking with the 3/3 Battle-Fan-equipped First-Time Flyer for lethal in the air; holding the Ally token back so their sick Patrol can't kill it for free.\n\
\n\
Rationale: The 3/3 flyer is exactly lethal (they're at 3), they have zero flying blockers and no cards in hand — their only possible answer is the Patrol's tap ability, which under the rules can't remove an already-declared attacker from combat, or a lucky one-card rip off a land-sac draw. This is the kill shot.",
